foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday August 12th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  the Federal Trade Commission is  launching an effort to expand online  privacy protections the regulator is  getting the ball rolling on a process to  create new rules targeting online  surveillance and lacks data security  practices by tech companies at a press  conference on Thursday FTC chair Lena  Khan spoke about why the agency was  taking this step the FTC for many years  over two decades now has really been on  the front lines of using our existing  enforcement tools to combat privacy  violations and data security breaches  but we've seen now that the growing and  continuing digitization of our economy  means that some of these practices may  now be prevalent and that case-by-case  enforcement may fail to adequately deter  law breaking or remedy the resulting  Harms when the rules do come out they  could fundamentally change the way our  data is gathered and used by tech  companies but while this is just the  beginning of the process the rules are  already facing criticism  joining us to discuss why that is and  what that could mean for our life online  is wsj reporter John McKinnon hi John  thanks for being here nice to be with  you so let's start with you this is  early days for the rulemaking process  but can you kind of lay out what the  goal for these rules is going to be well  the big goal is to just first figure out  if there is a real problem here in the  online environment that the FTC needs to  address so they're going to be taking a  lot of input and comment from the public  and from businesses and from advocacy  groups to try to figure out the extent  of the problems there are two main  problems here that they're trying to get  at one is whether people's privacies  really being violated in some meaningful  Way by all of the data collection and  targeted advertising and surveillance  that goes on on the internet and the  second issue is the question of whether  people's data is being handled in a way  that makes it vulnerable to hacking and  you know all the problems like identity  theft that flow from that  you who aren't as familiar with the FTC  and and its processes how far could this  rulemaking go I mean I realize now  they're just kind of looking at the  extent of the problem but were they to  make rules how extensive could they be  well they could ultimately go pretty far  I mean they've gotten petitions from  advocacy groups calling on them to for  example just ban surveillance  advertising in other words  individualized targeted advertising on  the internet  these groups say you know this is an  invasion of privacy and it leads to all  kinds of abuses  and therefore we ought to just get rid  of it  yeah I guess that gets to my next point  I mean who would be most impacted if  there were any changes here definitely  social media companies use of algorithms  to Target content to their users is one  big area that could be affected  but the biggest of all is certainly  advertising I mean and you're already  hearing sort of an outcry from industry  groups that are concerned that the  advertising economy could be upended by  this because it's so dependent on  people's data yeah let's talk a little  bit more about that criticism because  the FTC just announced on Thursday that  it was going to be making these changes  and already there has been some  criticism and what are critics upset  about well certainly the business  Community is concerned that this is  going to have you know tremendous  impacts on their advertising economy and  then you also are hearing from people  like the Chamber of Commerce that this  is basically something that is the  purview of Congress more than the FTC  and that the FTC just doesn't have the  authority to do some of the things that  they're going to be considering  yeah I mean when it comes to Congress  you know that was something that one of  the Commissioners Rebecca Slaughter  raised at the press conference on  Thursday until there's a law on the  books the commission has a duty to use  all the tools we have under current law  to address unlawful Behavior I see our  efforts as complementary  not an alternative to Congressional  legislation  how are Skeptics reacting to that and  how could these rules be complementary  well there is a concern that that the  FTC is going to do something that is  effectively going to discourage Congress  from acting so I mean it's hard enough  for Congress to to really do anything  and once you have you know other  government agencies  trying to do things that are pretty much  the same thing that Congress is trying  to do Congress has a tendency some  people say to just stop trying to do the  things that it's doing Congress has been  considering privacy legislation for  you know years but in the last six  months or so it's really gotten some  momentum and a comprehensive privacy  Bill actually passed the house Commerce  Committee a few weeks ago which is an  extraordinary step I mean I don't think  that it ever really happened before  and now you have the FTC trying to do  essentially the same thing so there's  you know some concern I think that this  FTC action is going to tend to sap some  of the momentum in Congress for doing  legislation  and Advocates I think particularly in  the business Community are are worried  that the FTC would go a lot further and  do a lot of things that Congress would  really never muster the political will  to do okay but time and time again on  this show when we talk about a law that  Congress is debating putting into place  when it comes to Tech everybody tells me  that like it's kind of a wait and see  game and it's hard to get Congress to  agree on anything so how realistic is it  that this privacy Bill turns into a law  I can't really give you odds but it is  definitely true that you know Congress  has gotten further in the last six weeks  then it's really ever gotten before so  yeah it's hard to say that this would  happen this year but it's very possible  that it could happen in the next year or  so and so I think that's one reason why  the FTC Commissioners today were very  careful to say you know what we're  trying to do is only complementary to  what Congress is trying to do and we're  not trying to you know usurpers to plant  them  all right that's our reporter John  McKinnon thanks for joining us John you  bet thank you  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang we  had production assistants from Ariana  osparu our supervising producer is Chris  zinsley our executive producer is  kateriokum and I'm your host Zoe Thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  [Music]

